  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes how myelin in the central nervous system can be renewed and remodeled through oligodendrocyte lineage dynamics. On the left, oligodendrocyte precursor cells (OPCs) are shown as a pool that can either proliferate or directly differentiate into mature oligodendrocytes. These newly formed oligodendrocytes may survive and integrate, or some appear to undergo death, highlighting turnover within the lineage. Arrows indicate that both direct differentiation and proliferation followed by differentiation contribute to generating new oligodendrocytes.\n\nThe center of the figure emphasizes “new oligodendrocyte integration and myelination,” suggesting that successful new cells become incorporated into tissue and begin wrapping axons. On the right, the consequences for myelin are illustrated. New oligodendrocytes can form entirely new myelin sheaths, while existing oligodendrocytes can also remodel myelin that is already present. The remodeling examples include sheath extension and retraction, as well as changes in sheath thickness around axons.\n\nOverall, the image presents myelination as a dynamic process driven by both the birth of new oligodendrocytes from OPCs and structural plasticity of established oligodendrocytes, rather than as a fixed one-time event.",
